A||N||5||gggg-us||Weight Transfer||||1||
Z||000000||gggg-us||12-20-2001||01:00 AM||||Ok, I've read a couple of other threads on the site, and different people have different things to say...but What I remember from Physics 1 in College was that force applied to a surface is the determining factor in traction...So wouldn't theoreticaly, the best traction come when your front tires are off the ground and all weight/force is applied to the rears. If I'm wrong then someone tell me why, but if I'm right, then why in the world would you move weight...ie battery, to the front, unless you were already pulling the front end off the ground?<P>------------------<BR>88 4Cyl ... Rebuilding 81 Vette Engine for it now.||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||12-20-2001||01:04 AM||||Most people are setting their cars up for overall handling, ie roadcoarse, not drag strip.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Blacktree||12-20-2001||01:08 AM||||Well...<P>When all weight is transferred to the rear (i.e. your car is doing a wheelie) you'll get great traction under acceleration. But of course you get no traction for steering or braking. That's great for drag racing, but sucks hardcore for autocrossing.<P>People like to move the battery to the front to help even out the front/rear weight distribution to aid in cornering and braking traction.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||honegod||12-20-2001||05:43 AM||||yah, I think the motivation is a desire to keep the front tyres in some slight contact with the pavement when the V8 urges them skyward.<P>for pure acceleration, you got it right, front end airborn and steer by leaning from side to side, not unlike a hang glider.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||12-20-2001||06:01 AM||||Exactly.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>Most people are setting their cars up for overall handling, ie roadcoarse, not drag strip.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||tesmith66||12-20-2001||07:31 AM||||The rearward weight transfer may give more traction, but the amount of torque required to pull the front wheels could be put to better use moving the car. The rotational force (torque) at the rear wheels wants to do two things: 1.)Propel the car forward and 2.)Rotate the front of the car upward. Since, in drag racing, the object is to get to the other end of the track as quickly as possible, you want the wheels to spend as much time as possible moving the car forward. That's why drag cars use four links, coil-overs and wheelie bars.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
